<p class="gna-lede">Ameloblastoma is a benign (non-cancerous) tumour of the jawbone that grows from cells which once formed tooth enamel. It does not usually spread to other parts of the body, but it is <strong>locally aggressive</strong> — it can expand through the bone, loosen teeth and change the shape of the face, and it tends to come back if any of it is left behind. For most types, the treatment with the lowest chance of recurrence is surgical removal with a margin of healthy bone, followed by reconstruction where needed and many years of follow-up scans.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-ameloblastoma">What is an ameloblastoma?</h2>
<p>Ameloblastoma is an <strong>odontogenic tumour</strong>, meaning it develops from tooth-forming tissue. It is uncommon overall, but it is one of the most frequently diagnosed odontogenic tumours. Studies from India and other parts of Asia and Africa report it among the commonest tumours of this group, and it is sometimes diagnosed only after it has grown large, often because a slow, comfortable swelling does not prompt an early visit.</p>
<p>About four in five ameloblastomas occur in the lower jaw (mandible), most often in the molar region and the ramus — the upright part of the jaw behind the back teeth. The rest occur in the upper jaw (maxilla), where they can extend towards the sinus and the eye socket. It is most often diagnosed in people in their twenties to fifties, although children and older adults can be affected.</p>
<p>Research has shown that many ameloblastomas, particularly those in the lower jaw, carry a change in a gene called <em>BRAF</em>. This has opened research into targeted medicines, but at present these remain under study and are not a substitute for surgery in routine care.</p>

<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ameloblastoma is benign but locally aggressive: it grows into surrounding bone and can recur.</li>
    <li>It is often not painful and may be found on a routine X-ray or as a slowly growing jaw swelling.</li>
    <li>A biopsy is essential — on X-ray it can look very much like a cyst.</li>
    <li>For the conventional (solid) type, removal with a margin of healthy bone carries the lowest recurrence risk.</li>
    <li>Larger resections are reconstructed, often with bone from the lower leg, to restore chewing and facial shape.</li>
    <li>Recurrence can appear many years later, so follow-up is long term.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="symptoms-diagnosis">Symptoms and diagnosis</h2>
<p>In its early stages, ameloblastoma usually causes no symptoms. As it grows, people may notice:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A slowly enlarging, firm swelling of the jaw, usually without pain</li>
  <li>A change in the shape of the face, or one side of the jaw looking fuller</li>
  <li>Teeth that drift, become loose or no longer meet properly</li>
  <li>Dentures that stop fitting</li>
  <li>Less commonly, pain, numbness of the lip or an ulcer over the swelling — all worth prompt review</li>
</ul>
<p>Diagnosis involves several steps:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Panoramic X-ray (OPG):</strong> ameloblastoma typically appears as a dark area in the bone divided into several compartments — sometimes described as a "soap-bubble" or "honeycomb" pattern — or as a single cyst-like space. The roots of nearby teeth may appear shortened (resorbed).</li>
  <li><strong>CBCT or CT scan:</strong> shows the full extent of the tumour, whether the outer layer of bone has been breached and how close it lies to the nerve, the sinus or the base of the skull. MRI is added when spread into soft tissue is suspected, particularly in the upper jaw.</li>
  <li><strong>Biopsy:</strong> a small sample examined under the microscope confirms the diagnosis and the subtype. Because a keratocyst, a dentigerous cyst and an ameloblastoma can look alike on scans, treatment is not planned on imaging alone. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/jaw-cyst-symptoms-types-treatment/">jaw cysts</a> explains how these conditions differ.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="types">Types of ameloblastoma</h2>
<p>The World Health Organization classification recognises several forms, and the type strongly influences treatment:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>How it is usually treated</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Conventional (solid or multicystic)</td><td>The most common form; grows within the bone with microscopic extensions beyond its visible edge</td><td>Resection with a margin of healthy bone</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unicystic</td><td>A single cyst-like space, more common in younger patients and often around an unerupted tooth</td><td>Sometimes treated more conservatively, depending on its microscopic pattern; close follow-up is essential</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Peripheral (extraosseous)</td><td>Arises in the gum rather than in the bone; rare</td><td>Local excision with a small margin</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Metastasising ameloblastoma</td><td>Very rare; looks benign under the microscope but has spread, usually to the lungs</td><td>Specialist multidisciplinary care</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A separate and rare tumour, <strong>ameloblastic carcinoma</strong>, is malignant and is treated as a cancer. Your pathology report will state clearly which diagnosis applies.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-recurs">Why does ameloblastoma come back?</h2>
<p>Conventional ameloblastoma does not have a neat, complete capsule. Small islands of tumour cells extend into the spaces of the surrounding bone, beyond the edge visible on an X-ray or at surgery. If the tumour is simply scooped out (enucleation or curettage), some of these islands can be left behind and slowly regrow.</p>
<p>Studies comparing approaches consistently report more recurrences after conservative removal of the solid type than after resection with a margin, although the exact figures vary from study to study. A recurrence can take a long time to show — often several years, and sometimes more than a decade after the first operation. This is the main reason surgeons discuss a more extensive operation even though the tumour is benign, and why follow-up continues long after the wound has healed.</p>

