<h2 id="treatment">Treatment: resection and reconstruction</h2>
<p>Every treatment plan is individual. It depends on the type and size of the tumour, where it is, your age and general health, and what matters most to you. A good discussion covers the trade-off openly: a smaller operation with a higher chance of the tumour returning, or a larger one with a lower chance.</p>
<h3>Conservative surgery</h3>
<p>Enucleation — sometimes combined with treatment of the cavity wall — or decompression to shrink a lesion first may be considered for some unicystic ameloblastomas, for people who are not fit for major surgery, or in children, where preserving the growing jaw is especially important. It requires disciplined follow-up because recurrence is more likely.</p>
<h3>Marginal resection</h3>
<p>When the tumour is limited and the lower border of the jaw is intact, the surgeon may remove the affected part of the bone with a margin while preserving a strip of the lower border. The jaw stays in one piece, so a large reconstruction is often not needed.</p>
<h3>Segmental resection</h3>
<p>For larger tumours, a full-thickness segment of the jaw is removed together with a margin of healthy bone — commonly around one centimetre or more — and any involved soft tissue. In the upper jaw, the equivalent is a partial maxillectomy. For the conventional type, this approach carries the lowest risk of recurrence.</p>
<h3>Reconstruction</h3>
<p>After segmental resection, the gap in the jaw is rebuilt, ideally during the same operation. A common method uses a <strong>free fibula flap</strong>: a section of the thinner bone of the lower leg, transferred with its own artery and vein, which are joined to vessels in the neck under a microscope. Bone from the hip (iliac crest) is another option. The new bone is fixed with titanium plates and can later support dental implants. Many surgeons now plan the resection and reconstruction on computer models with custom cutting guides — you can read more about <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a> and how <a href="/skin-grafts-and-flaps">flaps and grafts</a> are used in facial reconstruction.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-risks">Recovery and risks</h2>
<p>Recovery depends on the extent of surgery and varies from person to person.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Conservative surgery or marginal resection</strong> usually means a short hospital stay and a soft diet for several weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Segmental resection with a free flap</strong> is a major operation. A hospital stay of around a week or longer is typical, sometimes with a short period in intensive care. A temporary tracheostomy is occasionally used to protect breathing while swelling settles. Liquid and then soft diets are introduced gradually, and patients walk with support while the leg heals. Many return to desk work after several weeks to a few months.</li>
</ul>
<p>Risks to discuss with your surgeon include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Numbness of the lower lip and chin</strong> — in segmental resection the nerve running through the jaw often has to be removed with the tumour, so this numbness may be permanent. Nerve grafting is possible in some cases.</li>
  <li>Bleeding, infection or breakdown of the wound</li>
  <li>Partial or complete failure of a free flap — uncommon, but it may need further surgery</li>
  <li>Exposure or loosening of plates, sometimes needing removal later</li>
  <li>Changes in the bite, facial contour or mouth opening</li>
  <li>Effects at the leg donor site, such as ankle stiffness or weakness, which usually improve with physiotherapy</li>
  <li>Recurrence, even after well-performed surgery</li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on <a href="/blogs/recovery-protocols-jaw-reconstruction/">recovery after jaw reconstruction</a> describes the rehabilitation phase in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="follow-up">Long-term follow-up</h2>
<p>Because recurrence can be delayed, follow-up for ameloblastoma is measured in years, not months. A typical schedule involves clinical review with imaging (an OPG or CBCT scan) every six to twelve months for the first few years, then yearly. Many specialists recommend continuing for at least ten years, and some advise lifelong periodic checks. A recurrence detected early is usually smaller and simpler to treat.</p>
<p>Follow-up is also when dental rehabilitation is planned. Once the reconstructed bone has healed, dental implants with a fixed or removable prosthesis can help restore chewing and appearance. Timing varies, and some people choose to wait longer before implants so that early recurrence can be ruled out.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>See an oral and maxillofacial surgeon if you notice:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A firm swelling of the jaw that has been slowly growing, even if it does not hurt</li>
  <li>Teeth that have shifted or loosened without gum disease</li>
  <li>A dark area in the jawbone reported on a dental X-ray</li>
  <li>Dentures that no longer fit because the shape of the jaw has changed</li>
  <li>Numbness of the lip or chin</li>
</ul>
<p>If you already have a diagnosis, it is reasonable to seek a second opinion before a major operation. At Gnathos Facial in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, we review your scans and biopsy report, explain each option and its trade-offs, and plan surgery and reconstruction at NABH-accredited partner hospitals. Cashless insurance may be available for planned surgery, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation. Learn more about our <a href="/cyst-and-tumors-treatment">cyst and tumour treatment</a>.</p>
